<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Bulgarian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/681">681</a>–<a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Danubian Bulgaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Bulgarian Empire was a medieval state founded around 681 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. It became a leading Balkan power, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under Boris I in the 860s and, under Simeon I (893–927), became a centre of Slavonic literature written in Glagolitic and Cyrillic. Weakened by the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> in 968–971 and by Byzantine wars, it was conquered by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> by 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire.jpg" alt="First Bulgarian Empire" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iamnhaz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Bulgarian_Empire_Flag_Krum_Manasses_Chronicle_Render.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> were Turkic semi-nomadic warrior tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region. The Bulgars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into Scythia Minor, a nominal Byzantine province, in the 670s. In 680 the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iv">Constantine IV</a> led a large army and fleet against them but suffered a disastrous defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onglos">Onglos</a>, in or near the Danube Delta. The Bulgars crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and in 681 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> signed a treaty acknowledging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> as an independent state, ceding the lands north of the Balkan Mountains and agreeing to pay an annual tribute. It was the first state the Byzantine Empire recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Khan Sevar died in 753 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a> died out, and in fifteen years seven khans reigned and all were murdered. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> (r. 803–814) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> doubled in size and destroyed the Avar Khaganate. Boris I decided by 863 to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and in 866 he executed 52 leading nobles after an anti-Christian rebellion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-preslav">Council of Preslav</a> in 893 made Old Bulgarian the language of state and church.</p><p>Under Simeon I (r. 893–927) the empire reached its largest extent. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at Boulgarophygon in 896 and annihilated their army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomorie">Anchialus</a> in 917, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 923 and 924 without success. From 968 the Kievan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> invaded, and the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> forced him out in 971. In 1014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> crushed the Bulgarians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion">Battle of Kleidion</a> and ordered 15,000 prisoners blinded, sparing every hundredth man one eye to guide the rest home. By 1018 the last strongholds had surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Glagolitic alphabet, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> with their disciples in 886, and the Early Cyrillic alphabet was invented in the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>. Old Church Slavonic literature flourished under Simeon I and spread north, becoming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. The fully independent Bulgarian Patriarchate was recognised in 927. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madara-rider">Madara Rider</a>, a relief carved in a cliff, was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1979. The Second Bulgarian Empire followed in 1185.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Bulgarian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The eastern Avar lands were probably taken by the <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b> under Khan Krum early in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sviatoslav I (r. c. 945–972) took the Khazar fortress of Sarkel and sacked Itil, invaded <b>Danubian Bulgaria</b> in 968 and was defeated by the Byzantine emperor John I Tzimiskes at Dorostolon in 971; on his way home he was killed by the Pechenegs at the Dnieper rapids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>For more than a century and a half after Basil II completed the conquest of the <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b> in 1018, the lands between the Danube and the Balkan Mountains were Byzantine provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Khazars broke up that confederation in the later seventh century, some Bulgars moved west to the Danube, where they founded the <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b>, while others migrated north up the Volga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most 20th-century historians agreed that they came from the East along trade routes from the Byzantine Empire, and that the movement was greatly influenced by the Bogomils of the <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b>, perhaps through Paulicians resettled in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Tengrism was the prevailing religion of the Xiongnu, Huns, Xianbei, Göktürks, Bulgars, Yeniseians and Mongolic peoples, and a state religion of the First, Western and Eastern Turkic Khaganates, Old Great Bulgaria, the <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b>, Volga Bulgaria, Khazaria and the Mongol Empire.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
